1. Definitions

"Software" means HBA Guitar Taber: its source code, the desktop and web applications,
installers and builds, the models and weights trained for it, its user interface and
visual design, its documentation and design records, and the download website.

"Official builds" means the installers published by the author on the project's GitHub
Releases page: https://github.com/habaodunggithub/HBAGuitarTaber/releases

"Your content" means the recordings you put into the Software and the tabs, scores and
files it produces from them (for example .gp5, MusicXML, MIDI or ASCII tab).

"Commercial use" means any use intended for, or directed toward, commercial advantage
or payment: selling, renting or licensing the Software or access to it; including it
in, or bundling it with, a product or service that is paid for, carries advertising or
is offered as part of a business; running it to provide a transcription service to
others; or using it inside a company's internal operations.


2. What you may do (free of charge)

a. Download the official builds, install them on any number of devices you use, and run
   them for personal, educational, research or other non-commercial purposes. A person
   (a teacher, a student, a band member, a hobbyist) making tabs with it is
   non-commercial use of the Software, even if they later sell those tabs (section 3).
b. Read the source code on GitHub to learn how the Software works, check what it does,
   report problems and propose changes.
c. Build the Software from source and run your build on your own devices, for the same
   non-commercial purposes, without sharing that build with anyone.
d. Fork the repository on GitHub only to propose changes back to the author (pull
   requests).
e. Share the link to the official download page or the GitHub repository. Write about,
   review, film or teach the Software freely.


3. Your content is yours

The author claims no rights in your content. You may use, share, publish and sell the
tabs and files you make with the Software, including commercially, provided you have the
rights to the music and recordings they come from. This license does not give you any
right to music written or recorded by others.


4. What you may not do (unless the author agrees in writing)

a. Commercial use of the Software, as defined in section 1.
b. Redistribute the Software: do not re-host, mirror, upload or hand out its installers,
   builds or source code, modified or not. Share the official link instead (2.e).
c. Modify the Software and share the result, or publish any work derived from it.
d. Copy the Software's code, user interface, visual design (the musician's notebook
   look, illustrations, icon), documentation or trained models into another product, or
   use them to build, design or train a product or service that does what the Software
   does or competes with it. This applies whether the copy is exact, translated into
   another language, or closely imitated.
e. Use the Software, its source code, documentation or models to train or evaluate
   machine-learning models, other than running the Software as intended.
f. Remove or change copyright notices, this license or the author credit.
g. Use the names "HBA Guitar Taber" or "Taber", or the pick icon, for your own product,
   or in a way that suggests the author endorses you.
h. Break or work around any limit of the Software in order to do any of the above.


5. Contributions

If you send the author a change (a pull request, a patch, a suggestion with code), you
confirm you have the right to do so, and you give the author a perpetual, worldwide,
free and irrevocable license to use, change, license and sell it as part of the
Software or otherwise. You keep any other rights you had in it.


6. Third-party components

The Software uses libraries, models, fonts, sound fonts and tools made by others (listed
in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md, and in the files shipped next to those components, such as
FFmpeg's licence). They keep their own licenses. Nothing in this license limits a right
those licenses give you.


7. Commercial and other permissions

For commercial use, redistribution, or anything section 4 does not allow, ask the author
first: open an issue on the GitHub repository or write through the author's GitHub
profile. A permission counts only when the author gives it in writing.


8. Ending

This license ends automatically, for you, if you break it. If you stop the breach within
30 days of learning about it, and it is your first breach, your license is restored.
When it ends you must stop using the Software and delete your copies. Sections 3, 5, 9
and 10 stay in force.


